From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 02:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] spirv: EDEN_NX2_FRAG_HOT marks NaN and very large pixel shader colours

Core patch on mig020-core (the v1.219.020 series), a debug aid, off unless EDEN_NX2_FRAG_HOT=<limit>
is set. Not for a release.

The flashes follow one object and glow through bloom. This shows whether a pixel shader wrote the
bad value, and which pixels. A float colour component that is NaN, infinite or past <limit> is
stored as red 8, green 0, blue 8, alpha 1, per component. The flashes are yellow-white or cyan-white,
so look for any sign of the mark, not only magenta: a pixel hot in red or blue alone turns red or
blue, and hot green stores 0, so it goes dark. A mark on the object in the flash frame means the
pixel shader output is already bad, before any later pass. A flash that stays white means the value
stays under <limit> at that stage, or comes from a later pass or a compute pass, so run it again
with a lower limit. Try 4096, then 256. Normal HDR values may pass a low limit: compare with a frame
without a flash.

Each limit is new SPIR-V, so every value (4096, 256, unset) rebuilds every pixel shader, not only
the first launch. Do a warm-up launch per value, since scripts press at fixed times. Integer targets
are not looked at. Unset, nothing changes. Not built, not run.
---
 .../spirv/emit_spirv_context_get_set.cpp      | 21 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/shader_recompiler/backend/spirv/emit_spirv_context_get_set.cpp b/src/shader_recompiler/backend/spirv/emit_spirv_context_get_set.cpp
index 28f9869..01c2274 100644
--- a/src/shader_recompiler/backend/spirv/emit_spirv_context_get_set.cpp
+++ b/src/shader_recompiler/backend/spirv/emit_spirv_context_get_set.cpp
@@ -5,6 +5,7 @@
 // SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
 
 #include <bit>
+#include <cstdlib>
 #include <tuple>
 #include <utility>
 
@@ -13,6 +14,24 @@
 
 namespace Shader::Backend::SPIRV {
 namespace {
+// EDEN_NX2_FRAG_HOT=<limit> (unset or 0: off, a debug aid): a pixel shader's float colour that is
+// NaN, infinite or past <limit> is stored as a marker instead: red and blue 8, green 0, alpha 1,
+// per component, so a pixel hot in one component turns red or blue, and hot green goes dark. It
+// shows which pixels of a flash a pixel shader wrote as such, before bloom spreads them.
+Id MarkHotFragColor(EmitContext& ctx, u32 component, Id value) {
+    static const float limit = [] {
+        const char* const text = std::getenv("EDEN_NX2_FRAG_HOT");
+        return text != nullptr ? static_cast<float>(std::atof(text)) : 0.0f;
+    }();
+    if (limit <= 0.0f) {
+        return value;
+    }
+    const Id is_nan{ctx.OpIsNan(ctx.U1, value)};
+    const Id is_hot{ctx.OpFOrdGreaterThan(ctx.U1, ctx.OpFAbs(ctx.F32[1], value), ctx.Const(limit))};
+    const float mark{component == 3 ? 1.0f : (component == 1 ? 0.0f : 8.0f)};
+    return ctx.OpSelect(ctx.F32[1], ctx.OpLogicalOr(ctx.U1, is_nan, is_hot), ctx.Const(mark), value);
+}
+
 template <typename... Args>
 Id AttrPointer(EmitContext& ctx, Id pointer_type, Id vertex, Id base, Args&&... args) {
     switch (ctx.stage) {
@@ -491,7 +510,7 @@ void EmitSetFragColor(EmitContext& ctx, u32 index, u32 component, Id value) {
     const AttributeType type{ctx.runtime_info.color_output_types[index]};
     if (type == AttributeType::Float) {
         const Id pointer{ctx.OpAccessChain(ctx.output_f32, ctx.frag_color.at(index), component_id)};
-        ctx.OpStore(pointer, value);
+        ctx.OpStore(pointer, MarkHotFragColor(ctx, component, value));
     } else if (type == AttributeType::UnsignedInt) {
         const Id pointer{ctx.OpAccessChain(ctx.output_u32, ctx.frag_color.at(index), component_id)};
         ctx.OpStore(pointer, ctx.OpBitcast(ctx.U32[1], value));
-- 
